+  A uv environment is thousands of small files, slow to start from on a parallel filesystem and heavy on file
+  quotas. `"squash": true` (MCP `sync_environment(squash=true)`) packs it, after the sync, into one SquashFS file,
+  `.venv.sqfs` beside the then-empty `.venv`, with bytecode compiled; jobs mount the image read-only on `.venv`
+  (Singularity/Apptainer `--bind .venv.sqfs:.venv:image-src=/`), so every path in it is the one uv wrote and
+  the job sees an ordinary environment. It needs `mksquashfs` (squashfs-tools) where the server runs and a
+  Singularity or Apptainer sandbox; a setuid installation mounts the image with the kernel, an unprivileged one
+  needs `squashfuse`. A sync without `squash` unpacks the environment again and removes the image.
+
   Jobs only activate an environment, inside their sandbox (pixi's activation scripts included); they never
   install, so compute nodes need no network. The sync itself runs where the REST server runs, also in the
   sandbox, with only the project, the tool's cache and uv's Python directory writable, and the tool binary
